1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,410
If you average 100 km/h,
were you at exactly 100 at some point?

2
00:00:04,460 --> 00:00:11,030
Average-speed check: 20 km from camera A to B

3
00:00:11,080 --> 00:00:14,300
What if you got through in 12 minutes?

4
00:00:14,350 --> 00:00:18,270
The speedometer went anywhere from 60 to 140.
But was there a moment at exactly 100?

5
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:24,140
Let's graph the distance traveled

6
00:00:24,190 --> 00:00:26,540
The slope of the line from start to finish
= the average speed, 100

7
00:00:26,590 --> 00:00:31,110
The slope of the tangent = the speed at that moment
(the derivative from episode 1!)

8
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:34,180
A moment parallel to the average line!
The speedometer reads exactly 100

9
00:00:34,230 --> 00:00:37,350
If the drive is smooth with no jumps,
such a moment must exist = the Mean Value Theorem

10
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:41,050
So if your average is over 100,
you went over 100 somewhere

11
00:00:42,050 --> 00:00:45,800
NEXT: How do you find
the area under a curve?
